WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.120
Film Courage: Has there ever been a production
that’s been easy, where it looked easy and

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.120
it was easy?

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.280
Dale Stelly, Filmmaker (COMPTON’S FINEST,
SINGLE BLACK FEMALE and more): Hhmmm?

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.610
I’d like to think about that one for a second
because to be honest none of my movie productions

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.360
were easy.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:22.100
But if I had to pick one I’d probably pick
one of my first movies which is called THA’

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.100
CRIB.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.349
It was an urban comedy that went to Blockbuster
Video.

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:31.929
I produced that actually with my friend Daniel
Zirilli who I mentioned earlier.

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:38.230
He transitioned from music videos to the movie
business and being close and working together

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:44.350
I was able to get one of my first movies produced
with him.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.440
I remember it being a pleasurable experience.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.040
I remember working with a lot of supportive
actors and friends (we all came together).

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.800
That first one is usually special.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.350
I remember getting a lot of help with location.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:05.111
I remember my friend Neal Fredericks who shot
THE BLAIR WITCH PROJECT, he shot my movie

00:01:05.111 --> 00:01:06.890
for me.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.541
It was just a dope experience when I think
back on it.

00:01:09.541 --> 00:01:15.220
A lot of celebrities visited the set while
we were shooting at my parent’s house.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:22.350
Now that I think back on it, yes THA’ CRIB
was probably a fun shoot that didn't turn

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.000
into a headache.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.620
Sometimes these are supposed to be fun shoots.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:30.750
Sometimes when you’re dealing with money
and time and certain people not respecting

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:36.120
the creative process you have to deal with
certain things that take the fun out of it.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:45.950
But that one was fun, everything worked out
and on top of it I had no idea that was going

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.210
to go to Blockbuster and be everywhere that
it was so for me to be working full-time in

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.210
a post house.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:57.229
I think I was working at The Vault at the
time and my movie came out in Blockbuster

00:01:57.229 --> 00:01:58.910
was like pretty cool.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:04.049
I was like the all-star at the company for
a couple of years or whatever and I was putting

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:05.640
my movies out.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.619
People were trying to figure out how are you
doing this?

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:13.390
Like how did you shoot a movie and you are
working here full-time?

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.950
I just didn’t sleep a lot.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:24.280
And I found ways to shoot three movies without
losing my job at the post house.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:30.550
I used a lot of my vacation, I was strategic
with my vacation days.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:36.840
And I remember working night shift as a film
scan and recording operator and I remember

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.040
staying up for a period of almost eight days
straight.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.540
I think I stayed up three days straight and
then went to sleep and then stayed up another

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:52.980
three days straight and went to sleep because
my company wouldn’t let me take time off

00:02:52.980 --> 00:03:01.390
and I worked a night shift so I got my mind
mentally stabilized and I wanted to stay up

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:07.090
because they told me if I was unable to direct
my movie, they were going to get another director.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.760
But the movie wasn’t paying me enough to
quit my job.

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:17.020
So I had to make sure I kept my post job which
was obviously paying all my bills and everything

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:19.060
and this one movie.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.280
It was my third or fourth movie called SWEET
POTATO PIE.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.310
But I remember working a night shift.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:31.120
And I remember the way I worked it out I got
out at 6:00 in the morning and I had call-time

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:38.080
at 8:00 in the morning so I literally stayed
up…I made sure to start shooting on a Wednesday

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.170
because I was off on the weekends.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.810
So I stayed up Wednesday, Thursday, Friday
straight (I don’t know how I did it) but

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:52.390
my whole system was get off of work at 6:00
a.m., get home, shower, and I was ready for

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.610
8:00 a.m. call-time.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:04:00.489
Then I worked on set from 8:00 a.m. to 8:00
p.m. and then I was able to take a nap for

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:07.590
an hour or so to get to work for a 10:00 p.m.
because I worked from 10:00 p.m. to 6:00 a.m.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.880
And I did that literally for three days straight.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:17.809
I remember the only way I stayed up was I
had something to do every second every minute

00:04:17.809 --> 00:04:20.169
for the last 36 hours.

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:27.409
The job that I had required me to be attentive,
be with the computer with the film scan outs

00:04:27.409 --> 00:04:33.610
and I was on coffee and I remember having
my friend videotape me as proof that I was

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:39.639
pulling off this miraculous situation with
directing a movie that ended up going to Lionsgate.

00:04:39.639 --> 00:04:44.870
My first movie goes to Lionsgate and I’m
working in scanning and recording at the time

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.250
(it was 2002 or 2003).

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:55.129
But yes, it was a crazy feel when you are
passionate and dedicated and you have a chance

00:04:55.129 --> 00:04:58.229
and opportunity and I chose to keep both.

00:04:58.229 --> 00:05:04.499
I was like man I can’t quit my job because
I was making under $10,000 or something for

00:05:04.499 --> 00:05:05.499
doing a movie.

00:05:05.499 --> 00:05:14.259
It should have been way more than that but
yes, that was a pretty interesting situation

00:05:14.259 --> 00:05:16.990
when I pulled off the movie SWEET POTATO PIE.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:23.560
I did the urban version to the successful
film which was called AMERICAN PIE.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:29.710
So I was known for coming up with these spin-offs
of these successful films.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.569
Like SINGLE WHITE FEMALE, I did SINGLE BLACK
FEMALE.

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:36.150
PASSION OF THE CHRIST, I did JC IN THE HOOD.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:41.740
AMERICAN PIE, I did SWEET POTATO PIE and these
were titles that I picked and knew were going

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:46.189
to be sure shots with at least getting my
name and stuff out there.

00:05:46.189 --> 00:05:49.150
Because when you’re shooting you want to
make sure that you’re shooting things that

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.150
are going to sell.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:51.469
You don’t want to shoot things that aren’t
going to sell.

00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:57.230
So I always picked things that were super
successful and then I flipped it to be like

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:03.339
what would it be like if it was made in the
inner of Los Angeles, you know what I mean?

00:06:03.339 --> 00:06:06.460
That template for me worked a lot…it still
works actually.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:10.229
Film Courage: Would you actually walk into
Blockbuster?

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:12.210
How would you approach Blockbuster video?

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.819
Dale: Oh, no I would get a distribution deal
that would just put me everywhere.

00:06:16.819 --> 00:06:19.899
Film Courage: Oh I see…so you actually never
tried to go in.

00:06:19.899 --> 00:06:24.229
Dale: I never had to deal with the Blockbuster
executives or accounts or whatever.

00:06:24.229 --> 00:06:28.719
My distribution deal which was by a major
distributor had the relationships with the

00:06:28.719 --> 00:06:30.000
major studios.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.369
So once you sell your film to major studio
like Lionsgate, they make sure that it comes

00:06:34.369 --> 00:06:40.580
out on TV and DVD and everywhere that a movie
like that would be seen.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.990
Film Courage: How was that to go into Blockbuster
and see your film?

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.350
Dale: It was phenomenal because I remember
like one year I had three or four movies on

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:57.990
Blockbuster at one time and it was a crazy
feeling where it actually transitioned to

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.979
everything becoming digital and everything
being streamed online.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:05.819
At the time I had like three or four movies
at Blockbuster at one time and it was a very

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:12.629
good feeling to walk into Blockbuster and
see my movies next to like The Rock, Samuel

00:07:12.629 --> 00:07:15.780
Jackson or Will Smith.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:26.279
It was a pretty good feeling to see that my
hard work ended up on a major shelf and that’s

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:35.039
what gave me the energy to keep going more
and to shoot for bigger and better films and

00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:36.310
I’m still here.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:40.279
I’ve just got my first movie in a theater
(COMPTON’S FINEST) which we released through

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:46.460
Cinemark and it did pretty well and I’m
expecting to release more movies this year

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.419
independently through the theater.

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:51.379
I figured that out now.

00:07:51.379 --> 00:07:57.449
I figured it all the way out now from script
to the big screen as an independent production

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:03.800
company it’s very hard to…I don’t want
to say master…but to kind of figure all

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.409
that out.

